  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. It focuses on small-probability outcomes (less than 20%) and adjusts their weights upward by 10 percentage points. In Lottery A, the 25% chance of winning 3000 Euros is above 20%, so it is not considered a 'small probability' under this rule and remains weighted at 25%. In Lottery B, the 20% chance of winning 4000 Euros is exactly at the 20% threshold. Since the rule specifies 'less than 20%', this 20% probability is not adjusted. Therefore, no probability in either lottery qualifies for the upward weighting. The reason would thus rely on the standard expected value of small-probability outcomes, but since none fall below 20%, no adjustment occurs. The rule would effectively reduce to comparing the contributions of outcomes with probability <20%, which are absent here, so the reason may lead to indifference or reliance on other factors.",
